{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>My iniquity is greater than can be borne.</b> Once he saw that Elohim, blessed be He, exercises particular providence with absolute decisiveness, he thought that Elohim would know without doubt that he was not turning back from his sin out of remorse but only because of the punishment — and this, after the exhortation that Elohim, blessed be He, had testified to him to turn back in repentance, yet he remained obstinate. In this he said that his sin had no hope of atoning repentance that would shield him from the punishment he was now suffering — as with Saul in the matter of Amalek, when he said to Samuel, חָטָאתִי (1 Samuel 15:24), after the exhortation and admonition with which Samuel had roused him to turn back from his sin, and he was punished as stated: וַיִּמְאָסְךָ מִמֶּלֶךְ (1 Samuel 15:23)."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:29:08.047972116Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}